      "severity": "medium",
      "file": "t07.js",
      "line": 1,
      "evidence": "The function accepts an arbitrary 'term' string which is used to construct a RegExp without length validation.",
      "llm_reasoning": "While the regex is escaped, an extremely long 'term' string could still cause excessive processing time during the RegExp constructor or test phase if not constrained.",
      "suggested_fix": "Add a length check at the start of the function: if (term.length > 100) return [];"
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The code suffered from a critical performance bottleneck due to RegExp instantiation inside a loop and lacked necessary input validation and documentation. The refactored code optimizes the search logic, adds input length constraints to prevent potential ReDoS, and includes JSDoc for better maintainability.",
  "fixed_code": "/**\n * Filters lines based on a search term.\n * @param {string[]} lines - The array of strings to search.\n * @param {string} term - The search term.\n * @returns {string[]} - The filtered matches.\n */\nfunction findMatches(lines, term) {\n    if (!term || term.length > 100) return [];\n    \n    const matches = [];\n    const pattern = new RegExp(term.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\\\\]\\\\]/g, \"\\\\$&\"), \"i\");\n    \n    for (const line of lines) {\n        if (pattern.test(line)) {\n            matches.push(line);\n        }\n    }\n    return matches;\n}\n\nmodule.exports = { findMatches };"
}
